2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Maesa
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Maesa (7 May ca. 165 AD–ca. 3 August 224) was a Roman citizen[1] and daughter of Julius Bassianus, priest of the sun god Heliogabalus, the patron god of Emesa (modern Homs) in the Roman province of Syria. Grandmother of both
2 CONC  the Roman emperors Elagabalus and Alexander Severus, she figured prominently in the ascension of each to the title at the age of fourteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Saved Severan rule
2 CONT
2 CONT Like her younger sister Julia Domna, she was among the most important women to exercise power behind the throne in the Roman empire. Following the death of Caracalla, Julia Maesa rescued the Severan Dynasty from the usurper Macrin
2 CONC us.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Maesa was married to Syrian noble Julius Avitus and had two daughters Julia Soaemias and Julia Avita Mamaea each one mother of an emperor. Following the accession to the throne of her brother in law Septimius Severus, Julia 
2 CONC Maesa moved to Rome to live with her sister. After the murder of her nephew, the emperor Caracalla, and the suicide of Julia Domna, she was compelled to return to Syria. But the new emperor Macrinus did not proscribe her and allow
2 CONC ed her to keep her money.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once back in Syria and possessed of ample funds, Maesa engaged in a plot to overthrow Macrinus and place one of her grandsons, Elagabalus son of Julia Soaemias, in his place. In order to legitimise this pretension, mother and daug
2 CONC hter fomented the rumor that the 14 year old boy was Caracalla's illegitimate son. The two Julias were successful, mainly due to the fact that Macrinus was of an obscure origin without the proper political connections, and Elagaba
2 CONC lus became emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Chose Alexander Severus
2 CONT
2 CONT For her loyalty and support, Elagabalus honored Julia Maesa with the title Augusta avia Augusti (Augusta, grandmother of Augustus). The teenager proved to be a disaster as emperor, scorning Roman values with both religious and sex
2 CONC ual scandals. He took the liberty of marrying a Vestal virgin. She was one of a rumored five wives during his brief four years reign), Julia Maesa decided to promote instead her fourteen year-old grandson Alexander Severus.
2 CONT
2 CONT She convinced Elagabalus to adopt Alexander as his heir. Elagabulus was murdered shortly afterwards by the praetorian guard alongside his mother. Both were thrown into the Tiber river in contempt after being dragged from the palac
2 CONC e through the streets. This event followed a rumor that Alexander had died.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Julia Maesa=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Maesa (ca. 7 May 165 –ca. 3 August 226) was a Roman citizen and daughter of Gaius Julius Bassianus, priest of the sun god Heliogabalus, the patron god of Emesa (modern Homs) in the Roman province of Syria. Grandmother of bot
2 CONC h the Roman emperors Elagabalus and Alexander Severus, she figured prominently in the ascension of each to the title at the age of fourteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Restoration of Severan rule'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Like her younger sister Julia Domna, she was among the most important women to exercise power behind the throne in the Roman empire. Following the death of Caracalla, Julia Maesa rescued the Severan dynasty from the usurper Macrin
2 CONC us.
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Maesa was married to Syrian noble Gaius Julius Avitus Alexianus by whom had two daughters Julia Soaemias Bassiana and Julia Avita Mamaea each one mother of an emperor. Following the accession to the throne of her brother in 
2 CONC law Lucius Septimius Severus, Julia Maesa moved to Rome to live with her sister. After the murder of her nephew, the emperor Caracalla and the suicide of Julia Domna, she was compelled to return to Syria. The new emperor Macrinus 
2 CONC did not proscribe her and allowed her to keep her money.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once back in Syria and possessed of ample funds, Maesa engaged in a plot to overthrow Macrinus and place one of her grandsons, Elagabalus son of Julia Soaemias, in his place. In order to legitimise this pretension, mother and daug
2 CONC hter fomented the rumor that the 14-year-old boy was Caracalla's illegitimate son. The two Julias were successful, mainly because Macrinus was of an obscure origin without the proper political connections and Elagabalus became emp
2 CONC eror.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Appointment of Alexander Severus'''
2 CONT
2 CONT For her loyalty and support, Elagabalus honored Julia Maesa with the title Augusta avia Augusti (Augusta, grandmother of Augustus). The teenager proved to be a disaster as emperor, scorning Roman values with both religious and sex
2 CONC ual scandals. He took the liberty in marrying a Vestal Virgin (she was one of a rumored five wives during his brief four years reign), Julia Maesa decided to promote instead her fourteen-year-old grandson Alexander Severus.
2 CONT
2 CONT She convinced Elagabalus to adopt Alexander as his heir. Elagabulus was murdered shortly afterwards by the Praetorian Guard alongside his mother. Both were thrown into the Tiber river in contempt after being dragged from the palac
2 CONC e through the streets. This event followed a rumor that Alexander had died.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death & Reverence'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Maesa died on an uncertain date around 226. Like her sister Domna before her, she was deified.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_Maesa
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Julia Maesa (ca. 7 May 165 –ca. 3 August 226) was a Roman citizen[1] and daughter of Gaius Julius Bassianus, priest of the sun god Heliogabalus, the patron god of Emesa (modern Homs) in the Roman province of Syria. Grandmother of 
2 CONC both the Roman emperors Elagabalus and Alexander Severus, she figured prominently in the ascension of each to the title at the age of fourteen.
2 CONT Contents  [hide]  1 Restoration of Severan rule 2 Appointment of Alexander Severus 3 Death & Reverence 4 See also 5 References 6 External links Restoration of Severan rule[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Like her younger sister Julia Domna, she was among the most important women to exercise power behind the throne in the Roman empire. Following the death of Caracalla, Julia Maesa rescued the Severan dynasty from the usurper Macrin
2 CONC us.[2] Julia Maesa was married to Syrian noble Gaius Julius Avitus Alexianus by whom had two daughters Julia Soaemias Bassiana and Julia Avita Mamaea each one mother of an emperor. Following the accession to the throne of her brot
2 CONC her in law Lucius Septimius Severus, Julia Maesa moved to Rome to live with her sister. After the murder of her nephew, the emperor Caracalla and the suicide of Julia Domna, she was compelled to return to Syria. The new emperor Ma
2 CONC crinus did not proscribe her and allowed her to keep her money. Once back in Syria and possessed of ample funds, Maesa engaged in a plot to overthrow Macrinus and place one of her grandsons, Elagabalus son of Julia Soaemias, in hi
2 CONC s place. In order to legitimise this pretension, mother and daughter fomented the rumor that the 14 year old boy was Caracalla's illegitimate son. The two Julias were successful, mainly because Macrinus was of an obscure origin wi
2 CONC thout the proper political connections and Elagabalus became emperor. Appointment of Alexander Severus[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT For her loyalty and support, Elagabalus honored Julia Maesa with the title Augusta avia Augusti (Augusta, grandmother of Augustus). The teenager proved to be a disaster as emperor, scorning Roman values with both religious and sex
2 CONC ual scandals. He took the liberty in marrying a Vestal Virgin (she was one of a rumored five wives during his brief four years reign), Julia Maesa decided to promote instead her fourteen year-old grandson Alexander Severus. She co
2 CONC nvinced Elagabalus to adopt Alexander as his heir. Elagabulus was murdered shortly afterwards by the Praetorian Guard alongside his mother. Both were thrown into the Tiber river in contempt after being dragged from the palace thro
2 CONC ugh the streets. This event followed a rumor that Alexander had died. Death & Reverence[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Julia Maesa died on an uncertain date around 226. Like her sister Domna before her, she was deified. See also[edit]
2 CONT
