1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulvia_Plautilla
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Fulvia Plautilla=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Publia Fulvia Plautilla, Fulvia Plautilla or Plautilla (c. 185/around 188/189 - early 212) was the only wife of the Roman emperor Caracalla, who was her paternal second cousin. After her father was condemned for treason, she was e
2 CONC xiled and eventually killed on Caracalla's orders.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birth and family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Plautilla was born and raised in Rome. She belonged to the gens Fulvia of ancient Rome. The Fulvius family was of plebeian origin, came from Tusculum, Italy and had been active in politics since the Roman Republic. Her mother was 
2 CONC named Hortensia; her father was Gaius Fulvius Plautianus; the Commander of the Praetorian Guard, consul, maternal first cousin and close ally to Roman Emperor Lucius Septimius Severus (the father of Caracalla). She also had a brot
2 CONC her, Gaius Fulvius Plautius Hortensianus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Severus and Plautianus arranged for Plautilla and Caracalla to be married in a lavish ceremony in April 202. The forced marriage proved to be very unhappy; Caracalla despised her. According to Cassius Dio, Plautilla had a profliga
2 CONC te character.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to numismatic evidence, Plautilla bore Caracalla a daughter whose name is unknown in 204. In the same year, her father-in-law ordered the erection of the Arch of Septimius Severus, honoring himself and his family, includ
2 CONC ing his wife, Empress Julia Domna, Caracalla, Plautilla and her brother-in-law Publius Septimius Geta.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Exile'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 22, 205 Gaius Fulvius Plautianus was executed for treachery and his family properties were confiscated. Plautilla and her daughter were exiled by Caracalla to Sicily and then to Lipari. They were treated very harshly an
2 CONC d were eventually strangled on Caracalla's orders after the death of Septimius Severus on February 4, 211.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Contemporary depictions'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Coins bearing her image that have survived are mainly from the reign of her father-in-law. They are inscribed Plautilla Augusta or Plautillae Augustae.
2 CONT
2 CONT A marble bust of Fulvia Plautilla is in the Louvre.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Solinjanka or Salonitanka, meaning "woman from the city of Solin (ancient Salona)", is one of the most important Roman portraits found in Croatia, believed to depict Plautilla at a young age. Originally found in Salona, it is 
2 CONC now kept in the Archaeological museum in Zagreb.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulvia_Plautilla
