1 OCCU Seigneur de Vitry-en-Perthois, Roman Senator at Narbonne, Comte de Cologne, Pretender to the throne of Austrasia (532), seigneur de Vitry-en-Perthois (Lord of Vitry-en-Perthois), , Lord Vitry-en Parthois
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Munderic''' (c495-c532), Lord of Vitry-en-Parthois. He claimed to be son of [http://www.geni.com/people/Chlod%C3%A9ric-the-Parricide-King-of-the-Franks-at-Cologne/6000000008007946298 Clodéric "the Parricide"] bu
2 CONC t there is room for doubt. He was very young in 509 when his father was killed. He claimed the throne of Austrasia, rebelled against Thierry I who killed him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munderic
2 CONT
2 CONT Munderic (died 532/3) was a Merovingian pretender and claimaint to the Frankish throne. He was a wealthy nobleman and landowner with vast estates in the region around Vitry-le-Brûle (Vitry-en-Perthois now) near Châlons-sur-Marne. 
2 CONC The chief source for his life and deeds is Gregory of Tours.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 532 or 533 or around that year he put forth a claim to royal descent as being or claiming to be a son of Chlodoric the Parricide and asked for a share of the kingdom of Austrasia from Theuderic I. He had a band of sworn followe
2 CONC rs. Theuderic attempted to summon him to court in order to kill him, but after Munderic refused, a force was sent against him. The pretender took refuge with his loyal supporters in Vitry. The Austrasian army, however, lacked sieg
2 CONC e engines and were unable to seriously invest the place. Theuderic responded by sending a personal courtier of his, Arigisel, to negotiate for the rebels to come out, which they did. The dishonest ambassador had them cut down imme
2 CONC diately.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married a daughter of Florentinus (born 485), a Roman senator, and his wife Artemia, daughter of Rusticus of Lyons. They were the parents of Gondulphus of Tongeren, Bodegisel, Patrician of Provence, and Mummolin, possibly mayor
2 CONC  of the palace of Neustria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Bachrach, Bernard S. Merovingian Military Organization, 481–751. Minneapolis: University of Minnesota Press, 1971. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Settipani, Christian. Les ancêtres de Charlemagne. 1989. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
