1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Palatina of Troyes''' was born in circa 547 in Troyes ... in the Champagne region of France. She was the only known child of Maurilion Gallo, a Gallo-Roman aristocrat with likely ties to the Merovingian court. She married St. G
2 CONC ondolfus (Gundulphus), Bishop of Tongeren, and they had a son, Borogiso II of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gondulph_of_Maastricht
2 CONT
2 CONT Gondulph (Latin: Gondulfus, Gundulphus, perhaps also Bethulphus) of Maastricht, sometimes of Tongeren (6th/7th century AD) was a bishop of Tongeren-Maastricht venerated as a Roman Catholic and Eastern Orthodox saint. Together with
2 CONC  Saint Servatius and Saint Monulph, he is one of the patron saints of the city of Maastricht.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Legend 
2 CONT
2 CONT Very few facts are known about Gondulph of Maastricht. According to a legend, his parents were Munderic and Arthemia. He was married to Palatina, only known child of Maurilion Gallo, a Gallo-Roman aristocrat with likely ties to th
2 CONC e Merovingian court.[citation needed] By some reconstructions, the couple had a son named Bodegisel II, who became Duke of Aquitaine.[citation needed] He remains an enigmatic figure. It has been questioned whether he could be iden
2 CONC tical with Betulph, a bishop of Maastricht mentioned in 614 (see below).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Troyes-2
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia's short stub (with unreliable sources),[1] and various laundry lists of the genealogy of saints (in Google eBooks),[4] are contradicted, by Halfond (2010), who states that Palatina's father was Gallomagnus of Troyes. The
2 CONC  author further relates that she married, "Bodegiselus, an Austrasian dux." In the footnotes, Halfond (2010), goes on to say that he's citing Martindale's (1992), The Prosopography of the Later Roman Empire.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Information gleened from Martindale (1992), and elsewhere, seems to indicate that her husband may have been Bodegisel I[6][7] ... now thought, by some, to be Bodegisel II's uncle.[8][2] And while Settipani (n.d.), seems to back Go
2 CONC ndulphus, as a possible father of Bodegisel II, sources which cite the scholar, don't seem to mention his proposal for a mother.[9] Further research is needed to determine if she's the mother or aunt of Bodegisel II.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
