2 CONT
2 CONT > Only his will can give us information about Grimo's life. He then owed his education and care to the Episcopal Church of Verdun, to which he served as a deacon. However, Grimo was not dependent on church scholarships. His will d
2 CONC ocuments an immense amount of private property scattered across Chiers, Saar, Upper Moselle and Meuse north of the Ardennes, most of which was family inheritance. However, Grimo does not reveal who his parents were. He mentions hi
2 CONC s late sister Ermengundis, who also led a spiritual life as a deaconess (dyacona) . He calls a Duke Bobo his nephew. Whether it is the duke who died in 639 at the side of the young King Sigibert III. (around 630-656) fought agains
2 CONC t the Thuringian Duke Radulf (died after 642) is not certain. Grimo calls two other nephews sons of Ado (his brother?). An aunt on her father's side, who was buried in the later George's Monastery in Amay near Huy on the Meuse, wa
2 CONC s probably Oda (originally Chrodoara), who had been revered as a saint since the 11th century. She is said to have been married to an Aquitanian Duke Bodegisel (Boggis). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
