1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adalgisel_Grimo
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Adalgisel Grimo''' (died after 634) was a deacon and member of the Austrasian nobility. He is chiefly significant because of his will, dated 30 December 634. This is the oldest known early medieval deed for the territory betwee
2 CONC n the Meuse and the Rhine and contains important information about the settlement, constitutional, economic and social history of this region.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Adalgisel Grimo had a double name, such as appears occasionally in early medieval sources. Grimo is the diminutive of a longer polysyllabic name.[2] He was educated at the Cathedral of Verdun, served as a deacon under Bishop Paulu
2 CONC s of Verdun, and founded Tholey Abbey. He controlled a large territory between the Meuse and Rhine, which he bequeathed to St. Maximin's Abbey, Trier and the Monastery of Longuyon, among others.
2 CONT
2 CONT His will provides information regarding his family relationships. His sister was a deacon named Ermengundis. He mentions that his aunt, whose name is not given, is buried in the church of Saint-George in Amay. In 1977 the gravesto
2 CONC ne of Saint Chrodoara was found in this church. Chrodoara was married to a duke named Bodegisel, a member of a north Aquitanian aristocratic family. This helped confirm previous assumptions about the membership of Adalgisel in thi
2 CONC s family whose members are identifiable by names ending in "-gisil".[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT A 10th-century transcription of the Latin testament of Adalgisel Grimo resides in the State Archives in Koblenz (Rhineland-Palatinate).[4] The definitive edition of the text was presented in 1932 by Wilhelm Levison.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mummolin (Latin: Mummolinus) (b. circa 500) was a Frankish nobleman.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mummolin_of_Soissons
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a son or son-in-law of Munderic.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mayor of the Palace of Neustria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He fathered:
2 CONT *Bodegisel, murdered,[2] married to Chrodoare, of Amay. Some researchers claim that they were the parents of Saint Arnulf of Metz, but proof is lacking.
2 CONT *Babon, Duke, married and father of:
2 CONT **Ermengunde
2 CONT **Adon
2 CONT **..., married to ..., the parents of:
2 CONT ***Bobo, Duke between 634 and 641
2 CONT **Adalgisel Grimon
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # Wilhelm Levison, Das Testament Diakons Adalgisel-Grimo vom Jahre 634 In: Trierer Zeitschrift, Band 7, 1932, p. 69–85. < [http://www.mgh-bibliothek.de/dokumente/z/zsn2b004976.pdf PDF] >
2 CONT # https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adalgisel_Grimo his aunt, whose name is not mentioned, is buried in the church of Saint George of Amay. It is Oda von Amay (also: Chrodoara), whose empty sarcophagus was found in 1977 during archaeo
2 CONC logical excavations in the choir of the Romanesque collegiate church of Saint-Georges in Amay. 3][4] Oda was married to a duke named Bodogisel, the member of a northern Aquitaine noble family. 
2 CONT # https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oda_von_Amay Oda of Amay (also Chrodoara, * in the 6th Century; † before 634 in Amay) is a Franconian noble and Catholic saint. She was worshipped in the collegiate church of Saint-Georges in Amay.
2 CONT # https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrodoara Saint Chrodoara was a Merovingian noblewoman and traditionally the foundress of the Abbey of Amay, now in Wallonia, Belgium. Chrodoara is thought to have been born around the year 560 in S
2 CONC wabia.[1] She was probably married to Bodegisel-Bobo, the son of Mummolin of Soissons.[2][3] If so, she was widowed around 589. After the death of her husband she moved to Amay and devoted her wealth and her time to the church and
2 CONC  works of charity. She died sometime before the year 634 and was buried in the Church of Saint George in Amay. The church is now called "Saint George and Saint Ode", where Ode or Oda, the name dating from the eleventh century, is 
2 CONC identified as Chrodoara. Chrodoara is said to be the mother of [https://www.geni.com/people/Saint-Arnoul-Bishop-of-Metz/6000000003302337846 Bishop Arnulf of Metz], and she may have been the grandmother of either Hugobert or of his
2 CONC  wife Irmina of Oeren, and thus the great-grandmother of Plectrude, wife of Pepin of Herstal, but the evidence is too late to be relied upon.[4]
2 CONT * “Adalgisel Grimo, Deacon of the Episcopal Church of Verdun (died after December 30, 634)”  < [https://www-rheinische--geschichte-lvr-de.translate.goog/Persoenlichkeiten/adalgisel-grimo/DE-2086/lido/5f71ae51dcdc78.18700302?_x_tr_
2 CONC sl=de&_x_tr_tl=en&_x_tr_hl=en&_x_tr_pto=sc GoogleTranslate] > The existence of Adalgisel Grimo is only attested by his will dated December 30, 634, which has only survived in a copy from the 10th century. It is not known how old A
2 CONC dalgisel Grimo was at the time the will was drawn up. The document “is the oldest document of the early Middle Ages, the content of which touches the Rhineland [= the Prussian Rhine Province]”. It is a unique source for the eccles
2 CONC iastical, social, economic and linguistic history of Austria, the eastern part of the Merovingian Frankish Empire, in the early 7th century.  The deacon only calls himself in the introductory formula of his will, where he has to l
2 CONC egally declare his identity, with his given nickname Adalgisel and his acquired “pet name” Grimo ( Ego Adalgyselus qui et Grimo) . Further into the text he only speaks of himself as Grimo. While the name Adalgisel expressed to the
2 CONC  knowledgeable contemporary the deacon's position in his clan and thus his claim to a share of the property, Grimo was the everyday name with which he was addressed and with which he identified himself. Adalgisel Grimo is not a do
2 CONC uble name and therefore cannot be written with a hyphen. Only his will can give us information about Grimo's life. He then owed his education and care to the Episcopal Church of Verdun, to which he served as a deacon. However, Gri
2 CONC mo was not dependent on church scholarships. His will documents an immense amount of private property scattered across Chiers, Saar, Upper Moselle and Meuse north of the Ardennes, most of which was family inheritance. However, Gri
2 CONC mo does not reveal who his parents were. He mentions his late sister Ermengundis, who also led a spiritual life as a deaconess (dyacona) . He calls a Duke Bobo his nephew. Whether it is the duke who died in 639 at the side of the 
2 CONC young King Sigibert III. (around 630-656) fought against the Thuringian Duke Radulf (died after 642) is not certain. Grimo calls two other nephews sons of Ado (his brother?). An aunt on her father's side, who was buried in the lat
2 CONC er George's Monastery in Amay near Huy on the Meuse, was probably Oda (originally Chrodoara), who had been revered as a saint since the 11th century. She is said to have been married to an Aquitanian Duke Bodegisel (Boggis). Wheth
2 CONC er Grimo was related to Duke Adalgisel, who, together with Bishop  Kunibert of  Cologne  , took  over government affairs for the underage King Sigibert III in 633. took over, is still controversially discussed in research. What is
2 CONC  certain, however, is that Grimo belonged to a family whose representatives were able to interact on an equal footing with the most powerful men of Austria's Franconian elite, for whom the term nobility, strictly speaking, cannot 
2 CONC yet be used. This elite included Bishop Arnulf of Metz (served 614 – 629, died 643/647) and the caretaker Pippin the Elder. Ä. (died 640), whose children Ansegisel (602-679) and Begga (615-693) founded the Carolingian family.
